  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Fresh raw meat once rained out of a clear sky. In March 1876, a Kentucky farmer's wife watched red chunks pelt her yard for ten straight minutes.",
        "visual_query": "kentucky farm landscape 19th century",
        "visual_fallback": "rural farmhouse yard"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The pieces covered the grass and fences, smelling fresh and looking like beef or venison. Two daring locals even tasted it.",
        "visual_query": "vintage farm wooden fence field",
        "visual_fallback": "grassy pasture fence"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Laboratory tests confirmed it was real muscle, cartilage, and lung tissue. But with no aircraft invented and no storms nearby, what could possibly drop butchered meat across an entire acre?",
        "visual_query": "antique microscope laboratory science 1800s",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage science laboratory"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The bizarre answer finally came from vulture biology. When threatened or overloaded, flocks of soaring vultures will violently regurgitate their stomach contents in midair to lighten weight.",
        "visual_query": "flock of vultures flying sky",
        "visual_fallback": "birds soaring in sky"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A flock flying high overhead simply unloaded their recent meal simultaneously. But if dozens of vultures emptied their stomachs, how did not a single witness see the flock above?",
        "visual_query": "clear blue sky over farmland",
        "visual_fallback": "empty blue sky"
      }
    ],
